# Shatter — The rocks

This file defines the drifting rocks: their sizes, how they move, what a destroyed
rock leaves behind, and what the star does with one it swallows. What destroys a
rock and where its fragments are thrown are in `specs/collision.md`, and the waves
that put rocks on the field are in `specs/progression.md`.

## The three sizes

A rock is one of three sizes. Each collides as a circle of the radius below,
whatever it is drawn as.

| Size | `ROCK_RADIUS` | Base drift speed |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `large` | `46` | `ROCK_SPEED_MIN.large` to `ROCK_SPEED_MAX.large` (`60` to `110`) |
| `medium` | `26` | `90` to `150` |
| `small` | `14` | `130` to `210` |

A rock's base drift speed is the speed it enters the field with, drawn uniformly
from its size's range. The well then speeds it up and slows it down as it falls
toward and climbs away from the star, so its instantaneous speed varies from
there.

## How a rock moves

A rock drifts under momentum, is curved continuously by the well
(`specs/gravity.md`), and wraps at the field's edges carrying its velocity across.
Rocks pass through one another freely.

Each rock also spins slowly as it is drawn, for visual life. That spin is
cosmetic: it changes neither the rock's velocity nor its collision, so a rock
under no force other than the well moves exactly along the path the well and its
own momentum give it.

## Splitting

Destroying a rock produces the size below it:

| Destroyed | Leaves |
| --- | --- |
| `large` | Two `medium` |
| `medium` | Two `small` |
| `small` | Nothing |

Both fragments appear at the destroyed rock's position. The velocity each takes is
in `specs/collision.md`. Destroying a rock is the only thing that takes one off
the field, and since a `large` and a `medium` each leave two behind, the number
of rocks on the field falls only when a `small` is destroyed.

The two fragments are appended to the rock roster in order, each taking a fresh
id, and the destroyed rock's id is not reused while any live entity holds it.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}

## Armor

Every rock carries health, a whole number of hits it takes from the gun before it
is destroyed.

| Size | `ROCK_HEALTH` |
| --- | --- |
| `large` | `3` |
| `medium` | `2` |
| `small` | `1` |

- A bullet that hits a rock lowers that rock's health by exactly `1`, and the
  bullet is removed.
- While health remains the rock is not destroyed, does not split, and scores
  nothing.
- Only the hit that takes health to `0` destroys the rock, at which point it
  splits and scores as above.
- A fragment enters at full health for its size, so a `medium` fragment enters at
  `2` and a `small` at `1`.
- Health never recovers.

### Damage feedback

Each hit that leaves a rock standing produces a brief bright flash on the struck
rock lasting `HIT_FLASH_TIME` (`0.1` seconds), after which the rock returns to the
appearance its remaining health gives it.

A rock is drawn progressively more damaged as its health falls, so a player can
judge how many hits it has left. A rock at full health is drawn undamaged.
{{/if}}

## Star recycling

A rock the star swallows is the same rock relocated, not a fresh one.

When a rock's circle reaches the star's core (`specs/collision.md`), the rock is
taken from the core and immediately re-placed at the same size. It re-enters at a
point on one of the four edges of the field, the edge drawn with probability `1/4`
each and the point drawn uniformly along the whole length of that edge, heading
inward into the field, at a fresh base drift speed drawn uniformly from its
size's range.

The debug surface `specs/instrumentation.md` specifies poses the edge the next
recycled rock re-enters at, and the base drift speed the rocks of the next
placement take.

Recycling scores nothing and leaves the field's rock count unchanged.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "warhead")}}

A recycled rock carries exactly the health it had when the star took it, and it
re-enters drawn with the damage that health gives it. Its speed is reset and its
health is not.
{{/if}}
